DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061031T130000
SUMMARY:Good teaching in undergraduate mathematics
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061031T143000
DESCRIPTION:The next seminar will be\nTueday October 31st 1pm - 2.30pm i
 n C5CT1\n\nThe presentations will be\n\n\n\nGood teaching in undergradua
 te mathematics\n\nGrant Cairns\nHead of the Department of Mathematics an
 d Statistics\, La Trobe University\n\nAbstract:\nLa Trobe University has
  an established record of excellence in the teaching of mathematics for 
 over 2 decades\, and in recent years\, it has obtained stand-out perform
 ances in the CEQ results\, in both good teaching\, and overall student s
 atisfaction. Underlying this reputation is its unique teaching philosoph
 y and original unit organization\, which places the emphasis on active s
 tudent participation. We will describe how this practice has been enable
 d and maintained by the development of integrated course materials\, the
  articulation of the various parts of the unit structure (tutorials\, pr
 actice classes\, computer labs\, lectures\, etc)\, the formation of the 
 teaching staff\, and the continuous process of unit review.\n\n\nGroup L
 earning Exercise\nProfessor Roger Parish\, La Trobe University\n\nThe ex
 ercise was developed to address the persistently poor examination perfor
 mance in a second year biology subject after more conventional methods (
 tutorials\, essays\, etc.) had failed.  The goals of the exercise are :\
 n.	To increase the understanding of material presented in lectures.\n.	T
 o provide an opportunity to reflect upon the background and future impli
 cations of the information obtained.\n.	To communicate what students bel
 ieve to be the most important aspects of the material to others.\n.	To r
 eceive information from other members of the group and discuss that info
 rmation within the group.\n\nStudents work in independent groups of thre
 e\, each member dealing with a component of the lecture material and rel
 ated additional reading with which they have been provided.  The approac
 h has now proved successful with large first and second year classes\, d
 eveloping various generic skills as well as increasing understanding of 
 the material.\n\n
END:VEVENT
