LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20060918T113222Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:11
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20060920T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20060920T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:The next meeting of the Australian Category Seminar will tak
 e place at Macquarie University on Wednesday 20th of September  2006\, i
 n the seminar room E7A 333.\n\nThe talks will be as follows:\n\n--------
 ------------------------------------------------------------------------
 ----\nAt 2:05pm\n\nSpeaker: Frank Valckenborgh (Macquarie)\n\nTitle:  \"
 On the Structure of Observables and Symmetries in Quantum Logic\"\n\n\n\
 n-----------------------------------------------------------------------
 -------------\n4:00pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker: Alexei Davydov\n\nTitle:  Com
 posing monoidal auto-equivalences\n\nAbstract: In some cases monoidal eq
 uivalences between representation categories can be described by means o
 f Drinfeld twists. In some cases isomorphism classes of twists posses ne
 at classification. We will try to show how the composition works on the 
 level of (classes) of twists.\n\n
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20061004T023521Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:12
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061004T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061004T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:At 2:05pm\n\nSpeaker: Ross Street (Macquarie)\n\nTitle:  \"W
 eak bimonoids in braided monoidal categories.\"\n\n4:00pm (approx)\n\nSp
 eaker: Dorette Pronk (Dalhausie\, Macquarie)\n\nTitle:  \"Bicategories o
 f Fractions.\"\n
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20061031T055540Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:15
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061101T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061101T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:At 2:05pm\n\nSpeaker: Ross Street (Macquarie)\n\nTitle:  \"K
 an extensions in basic group representation theory.\"\n\n---------------
 -------\n4:00pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker: Dorette Pronk (Dalhausie\, Macquari
 e)\n\nTitle:  \"Conformal Field Theory as a Nuclear Functor\"\n(Joint wo
 rk with Prakash Panangaden and Rick Blute)\n\nAbstract: \nSegal's defini
 tion of a conformal field theory discusses\n\"functors\" for which the d
 omain is not a category. There is\nan associative notion of composition 
 or arrows\, but there\nare no identities. Segal claim that this is not i
 mportant\,\nbut we claim that one should not just freely add identities.
 \nSegal's original category can be seen as a nuclear ideal\nin a larger 
 category and the functors one wants to consider\nas conformal field theo
 ries are nuclear functors. We will\npresent one example of such a functo
 r\, due to Neretin.\n\n\n\n---------------------------------------------
 ---------------------------------------\nWe will have lunch in the Macqu
 arie Staff Club. We leave from E7A Level 3 at 12.45 pm.\n\nInformation o
 n previous talks and other things related to the seminar can be found on
  the AusCat web page:\n<http://www.maths.usyd.edu.au/u/stevel/auscat/>
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20070305T065140Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:18
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20070307T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20070307T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:At 2:05pm\n\nSpeaker:  Clemens Berger (University of Nice)\n
 \nTitle:  \"Small CW-models for Eilenberg-MacLane spaces.\"\n\nAbstract:
  In this talk\, I shall explain how to construct out of Joyal's cell cat
 egory Theta comparatively small CW-models for Eilenberg-MacLane spaces. 
 The cellular structure is completely encoded by some tree combinatorics.
  In particular\, Serre's calculations of the cohomology mod 2 of K(Z/2\,
 n) can be done on cochain level.\n\n------------------------\n4:00pm (ap
 prox)\n\nSpeaker: Steve Bennoun (Macquarie)\n\nTitle:  \"Tannaka Duality
  for Comodules over a Hopf Algebra.\"\n\nAbstract :\nIn this talk\, I sh
 all first define the category of comodules over a bialgebra A and prove 
 that it is autonomous when A is a Hopf algebra. Then\, starting with thi
 s category\, I shall use Tannaka duality to reconstruct the bialgebra A.
  This will naturally lead to a construction for freely adjoining  an inv
 ertible antipode to a bialgebra.\n
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20061219T024851Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:18
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061220T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061220T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:At 2:05pm\n\nSpeaker: Glynn Winskel (University of Cambridge
 )\n\nTitle:  \"Event structures with symmetry.\"\n\n--------------------
 -----------\n4:00pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker: Pawel Sobocinski (University of
  Cambridge)\n\nTitle:  Distributed transactions and reversibility (joint
  work with\nVincent Danos and Jean Krivine)\n\nAbstract:\nA distributed 
 transaction is a computation where several agents agree on a common acti
 on. Such agents normally do not have a global view of the state space an
 d therefore must come to agreement by local (typically one-to-one) inter
 actions. When modelling such behaviour\, it is normally straightforward 
 to guarantee soundness -- a transaction occurs only if the individual ag
 ents satisfy the correct local requirements.\nCompleteness is often much
  more difficult\; indeed\, the existence of a\npossible computation lead
 ing to the required global state does not\nguarantee that such a state w
 ill be reached. For instance\, the agents\nmay deadlock while racing to 
 obtain the necessary shared resources.\nCompleteness is normally achieve
 d by including explicit backtracking\nmechanisms in the model.\n\nWe sha
 ll discuss how to model distributed transactions in such models by avoid
 ing ad-hoc implementations of backtracking. The solution is described at
  a general categorical level\, meaning that it can be exploited in proce
 ss calculi\, Petri nets and graph transformation\nsystems alike. The  ma
 in underlying structure is a factorisation system\nwhich allows a comput
 ation to be broken up into an irreversible\ncomponent followed by a reve
 rsible component. The construction is proved to be correct by exhibiting
  a certain equivalence of categories. In the concrete case of Petri nets
 \, the construction can be visualised nicely\nby utilising string diagra
 ms.\n-------------------------------\nWe will have lunch in the Macquari
 e Staff Club. We leave from E7A Level 3 at 12.45 pm.
END:VEVENT
