BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20070508T045945Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:25
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20070509T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20070509T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:**Please volunteer for a talk tomorrow. We only have a reluc
 tant candidate for the second talk. Let me (Ross\, in the absence of Ale
 xei) know as soon as possible whether you would like to speak. However\,
  the Seminar is definitely on!**   \n\nThe next meeting of the Australia
 n Category Seminar will take place at Macquarie University on Wednesday 
 9 May  2007\, in the seminar room E7A 333.\n\nThe talks will be as follo
 ws:\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------------
 -------------------\nAt 2:05pm\n\nSpeaker:  Jon Cohen (ANU)\n\nTitle: \"
 Computational coherence\"\n\nAbstract:\nIterated monoidal categories for
 m a categorical model of iterated loop spaces. The original paper on the
 se structures included an intricate proof of coherence of the \"every di
 agram commutes\" flavour. From a computational perspective\, what makes 
 the coherence theorem nontrivial is that the      underlying term rewrit
 ing system is non-confluent and\, as such\, does not enjoy unique normal
  forms. We'll begin with a brief introduction to iterated monoidal categ
 ories and use this as motivation for reducing coherence problems to ques
 tions in term rewriting theory. Within this setting\, we'll derive suffi
 cient conditions for the positive resolution of various coherence proble
 ms even in cases where the rewriting system is non-confluent and/or non-
 terminating. With this machinery in hand\, we'll return to iterated mono
 idal categories and give a more conceptual approach to their coherence p
 roblem.\n\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------
 -------------------------\n4:00pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker and Title:  To be 
 announced\n\n-----------------------------------------------------------
 -------------------------\nWe will have lunch in the Macquarie Staff Clu
 b. We leave from E7A Level 3 at 12.45 pm.\n\nInformation on previous tal
 ks and other things related to the seminar can be found on the AusCat we
 b page:\n<http://www.maths.usyd.edu.au/u/stevel/auscat/>\n\nRoss
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20070820T062951Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:31
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20070822T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20070822T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:The next meeting of the Australian Category Seminar will tak
 e place at Macquarie University on Wednesday 22nd of August  2007\, in t
 he seminar room E7A 333.\n\nThe talks will be as follows:\n\n-----------
 -----------------\nAt 2:05pm\n\nSpeaker:  Mark Weber (Macquarie)\n\nTitl
 e:  \"Higher operad algebras as enriched categories.II\"\n\n------------
 ----------------\n4:00pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker:  Bob Rosebrugh (Mount Alli
 son University)\n\nTitle:  \"Two or three views on database views.\"\njo
 int with Michael Johnson and Richard Wood\n\nViews are important in info
 rmation system design and management. The view update problem: `when can
  a change in a view state be extended to the total database state?' has 
 been much studied. An early approach treated states as unstructured sets
  and advocated views with `constant complement'\, although `constant fac
 tor' would have been a better name.\nRecently the `lens' concept and a s
 imple monad have elucidated the\nsituation. A more recent order-based ap
 proach with an ordered set of states provides a generalization though st
 ill requiring complements. When\nstates are a category of models\, a les
 s narrow view of updatability\nemerges.\n\nWe will have lunch in the Mac
 quarie Staff Club. We leave from E7A Level 3 at 12.45 pm.
END:VEVENT
