UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:44
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080319T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080319T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:Dear Categorists\,\n\nMy name is Tom Booker and as Dr Davydo
 v has decided to pass the cup of organization I shall be the new conveno
 r for the weekly CoACT seminar held at Macquarie University\, Sydney. If
  you have any questions concerning the seminar (or anything else for tha
 t matter) please feel free to contact me and I shall ensure that they ar
 e answered in a timely (and correct!) manner. \n\nThe next meeting of th
 e Australian Category Seminar will take place at Macquarie University on
  Wednesday 19th of March 2008\, in the seminar room E7A 333.\n\nThe talk
 s will be as follows:\n\n------------------------\n\n->   2:05pm (approx
 )\n\nSpeaker: Dominic Verity\n\nTitle: \"Cobordisms and Weak Complicial 
 Sets\"\n\nAbstract:\n\n	It is generally held that the totality of all n-
 manifolds (with corners) should support a canonical weak n-categorical s
 tructure\, under which all composites of cells are describable as \"glui
 ngs\" of manifolds along common boundary components. At low dimensions\,
  not only do we know that this is indeed the case\, but we also know how
  to describe free weak 2- or 3-categories in terms of such higher catego
 ries of \"cobordisms embedded in cubes\".\n\nIn this talk we describe ho
 w to build a weak complicial set (qua simplicial weak \\omega-category) 
 Cob^k\, whose n-simplices correspond to embeddings of manifolds of some 
 fixed co-dimension k into the geometric n-simplex. Here we work in the P
 L-category in order to avoid some of the technicalities involved when wo
 rking with manifolds with corners in the smooth setting. However\, it is
  likely that our main results bear direct translation to that latter con
 text.\n\nFrom the point of view of the theory of weak complicial sets\, 
 Cob^k is an interesting structure for a number of reasons. Firstly\, its
  underlying simplicial set is a Kan complex. Secondly\, it possess a str
 atification which makes it into a weak complicial set but which is non-t
 rivially distinct from the \"all simplices are thin\" structure that com
 es with Kan-ness. Finally\, we have strong reasons to conjecture that th
 is non-trivial complicial stratification can be extended\, by making thi
 n all simplices which are \"morally\" equivalences\, to a complicial str
 atification whose thin simplices bear a characterisation in terms of sim
 ple homotopy equivalences (and which are thus s-cobordism-like).\n\n----
 --------------------------->   4:00pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker: Boris Chorny\
 n\nTitle: \"Smallness in the model category and smallness in the homotop
 y category\"\n\nAbstract:\n\n	The concept of smallness in homotopy theor
 y generalizes the concept of compactness from classical topology. Howeve
 r\, there are two possible generalizations of this notion:  one is used 
 in the model category theory\, whether the other one is used in the real
 m of triangulated categories. The relation between these two concepts re
 mained subtle for a long time. Mark\nHovey has shown in his book on Mode
 l categories that the smallness in a stable finitely generated category 
 implies smallness in its homotopy category. Recently Rosicky generalized
  this result to combinatorial model categories.\n\nIn this talk we will 
 exhibit an example of a model category Quillen equivalent to the categor
 y of spaces with the following property: every homotopy type has a count
 ably small representative. In particular\, smallness in this model categ
 ory does not translate into smallness in the homotopy category. Our exam
 ple stems out of the work on enriched Brown representability. Connection
 s with homotopy calculus and orthogonal\ncalculus will also be discussed
 .\n------------------------\n\nKind Regards\nTom\n
END:VEVENT
