BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20080407T015431Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:46
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080409T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080409T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:	Dear Categorists\,\n\nAnother week another seminar! I would
  like to take this opportunity to thank Dr Isar Stubbe\, who has been wi
 th us here at Macquarie for a number of weeks now with his lovely girlfr
 iend (they both left this Sunday just past). Dr Stubbe has given numerou
 s talks at the last couple of seminars and we are greatly appreciative f
 or his time and effort. In particular the seminar he shared with Profess
 or Peter Johnstone\, who also visited us a couple of weeks ago. Thank yo
 u again to both of them!\n\nThe next meeting of the Australian Category 
 Seminar will take place at Macquarie University on Wednesday 9th of Apri
 l 2008\, in the seminar room E7A 333.\nAs always we shall be meeting at 
 the front of Professor Street's office at 12:45pm and then departing as 
 a group to the staff club for lunch.\n\nThe talk will be as follows:\n\n
 ------------------------\n\n->   2:05pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker: Mr Micah Bl
 ake McCurdy\n\nTitle: \"When is a linearly distributive category pivotal
 ?\"\n\nAbstract:\n	A linearly distributive category with negation can be
  thought of as a suitably generalised pivotal category\, where the singl
 e dualising operation of a pivotal category is teased into two. Joyal an
 d Street\, in their treatment of pivotal categories\, and have shown tha
 t every pivotal category is equivalent to one whose dualising operation 
 is strict\; this result can be generalised to the case of linearly distr
 ibutive categories with negation to produce retract equivalences between
  arbitrary such and \"strict\" ones\, which have strict dualising operat
 ions\, and whose de Morgan isomorphisms are identities.\nFurthermore\, i
 f one has lying to hand a cyclic structure on such an\nLDC\, one can ask
  what conditions must be placed on it so that the\nobvious candidates fo
 r (one-sided) pivotal structures are\, in fact\,\npivotal. As a pleasant
  complement to the previous result\, which said\nthat a cyclic structure
  represented a twist on a monoidal category if\nand only if it respected
  the tensor structure\, here we find a pivotal\nstructure if and only if
  the cyclicity respects the par structure.\n\nRegularly scheduled beer w
 ill resume\, to celebrate the joyous easter season.\n\n-----------------
 -------\n\n->   4:05pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker: Professor Ross Street\n\nTit
 le: \"Quantum Presheaves Part II\" (A joint work with Mr Dimitri Chikhla
 dze)\n\n------------------------\n\nI hope to see you all there!\n\nKind
  Regards\nTom\n
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT5H
LOCATION:Staff Club then E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20080506T060340Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:47
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080507T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080507T124500
SUMMARY:Category Seminar
DESCRIPTION:2:05pm (approx)\n\nSpeaker: Mr Michael Shulman\n\nTitle: \"E
 nriched derived functors and homotopy limits\"\n\nAbstract:\n\n       On
 e very general definition of \"homotopy theory\" is using strict\nthings
  to get information about weaker ones\; for example\, using\n2-categoric
 al tools to prove bicategorical theorems.  The passage from\ncategories 
 of strict objects to categories of weak ones is fairly well\nunderstood\
 , but the same process applied to functors still hides some\nmysteries. 
  We'll start by reviewing several notions of \"derived\nfunctor\" and ho
 w to describe them abstractly\, and then discuss how to\ngeneralize them
  to an enriched situation.  The traditional context is\nQuillen's theory
  of model categories\, but in fact considerably less\nstructure suffices
 .  Finally\, we'll apply these ideas to construct\,\nand abstractly char
 acterize\, weighted homotopy limits.\n
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
LOCATION:Staff Club\, E6A 102 and E7A 333
DTSTAMP:20080611T011437Z
UID:0806F75C-BB4E-4FED-B703-2B9FD8871511
SEQUENCE:53
RECURRENCE-ID;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080611T124500
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080611T120000
SUMMARY:Category Seminar and Colloquium
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080611T174500
DESCRIPTION:Dear Categorists\,\n \nI would like to blame my mess up on t
 he University's email system but alas\, it was my own.\nTomorrow (Wednes
 day) our usual lunch time of 12.45pm overlaps the Macquarie Mathematics 
 Colloquium.\nPlease note the modified sceduale as follows:\n \n- 12:00pm
  Combined Colloquium/Category lunch at the Staff club. We will be depart
 ing from the tea room on level 4 of E7A.\n \n- 1:00pm Mathematics colloq
 uium. CoACT members are encouraged to join in!\n \n- 2:00pm The Australi
 an Category Seminar commences as usual.\n \nI apologize for any inconven
 ience caused.\nUnfortunately I shall not be attending the lunch or the c
 olloquium due to teaching obligations\, but I will see you all at the se
 minar as usual!\n \nKind Regards\nTom Booker\nMATHEMATICS COLLOQUIUM\n\n
 Date: Wednesday 11 June 2008\n\nTime: 1pm\n\nPlace: E6A102\n\nSpeaker: D
 ouglas Rogers\n\nTitle: The Triumph of Mathematics in Australia\; or\n``
 It's the Mathematicians\, Stupid''.\n\nDedication: To the memory of Pete
 r Arthur Bsrry Pleasants\n          24:VIII:1939 -- 20:IV:2008\n\nIt may
  only be human nature that people lament their lot. But\nperhaps mathema
 ticians in Australia today are too close to the\naction to appreciate th
 eir achievement.\n\nFifty years ago\, in 1958\, mathematics faculty at t
 he University of New England (UNE) set up the New England Mathematical\n
 Association (NEMA) to promote dialogue between secondary and\ntertiary s
 ectors. But\, by November\, 2007\, the few remaining\nmathematics facult
 y at UNE found they had been left out of the\nNational Summer School for
  mathematics teachers hosted in Armidale by colleagues at UNE with a bud
 get of AUD5m\, although science faculty were included in a comparable ac
 tivity for science teachers\, notwithstanding that was being held in Sou
 th Australia. NEMA itself has almost ceased to function.\n\nWhen an arti
 cle dedicated to the fiftieth anniversary of NEMA and\nsubmitted to the 
 Gazette of the Australian Mathematical Society\n(AustalMSGaz)\, it was c
 onsidered a reason to reject that ``the level\nat which the readership o
 f this paper is directed seems ... to be the secondary audience rather t
 han the tertiary'' (suggesting that pupils\nat secondary school in Austr
 alia must be familiar with the\nBolyai-Gerwien Theorem). The rejection w
 as passed on by the Editor\, a member of faculty at the University of So
 uthern Queensland (USQ) where\, as at UNE a few years earlier\, halving 
 the number of faculty lines in mathematics had been proposed\, despite a
 n inovative mathematics\neducation course being in the works at USQ.\n\n
 These developments will resonate with mathematicians at Macquarie\nUnive
 rsity. When the speaker visited in March\, 2007\, he gave talks to both 
 mathematicians and mathematics educators. Several of the mathematics fac
 ulty took him aside to voice their suspicions about mathematics educator
 s\, but none attended his session with the\nmathematics educators\, not 
 least because it was found that none had\nthought to send round a notice
 \, until prompted by the speaker. Coming through again in November\, 200
 7\, the mathematics faculty saw no benefit to themselves in helping set 
 up another session with the mathematics educators\, and not surprisingly
  nothing eventuated.\n\nAs it happens\, the article rejected by the Aust
 ralMSGaz had already\nbeen accepted in extended form for publication in 
 The Mathematical\nGazette\, a long-established\, internationally recogni
 sed periodical with a backlog of some two-years. The purpose of the talk
  is to place the triumph of mathematics in Australia in context by revie
 wing the contents of the article rejected in Australia but accepted inte
 rnationally.\n\n\n
END:VEVENT
