BEGIN:VEVENT
LOCATION:E6A 133
DTSTAMP:20061009T003655Z
UID:BAE15ACF-678C-4B9F-A5D5-3AF2B51F5117
SEQUENCE:8
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061016T120000
SUMMARY:MATHEMATICS COLLOQUIUM
DTEND;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20061016T140000
DESCRIPTION:Lunch at Staff Club at noon.\n\nTalk:\n\nTime: 1pm\n\nPlace:
  E6A 133\n\nSpeaker: Robert Smith\nMcMaster Univ.\n\nTitle: Adherence to
  antiretroviral HIV drugs: how many doses can you miss before resistance
  emerges?\n\nAbstract.\nThe emergence of drug resistance is one of the m
 ost prevalent reasons for\ntreatment failure in HIV therapy. Drug resist
 ance is facilitated in large\npart by a patient's degree of adherence to
  prescribed therapy. A\nmathematical model is developed to quantify the 
 relationship between drug\nlevels and resistance\, using impulsive diffe
 rential equations to model the\nnature of drugs. Since drug levels may b
 e low\, intermediate or high\, the\ncorresponding model of virus and T c
 ells will vary as the drugs traverse\ndifferent regions. It can be shown
  that intermediate drug levels will\nfacilitate the emergence of drug re
 sistance while providing no measurable\nincrease in the T cell count for
  the patient. Only when drug levels are\nsufficiently high will the viru
 s be controlled. Parameter space of drug\ndosages and dosing intervals i
 s explored\, in order to prescribe dosing\nintervals and dosages to prev
 ent or reduce resistance. The model is then\nused to quantify adherence\
 , which has been described by the U.S.\nDepartment of Health and Human S
 ervices as the most urgent unanswered\nquestion in HIV research. Drug th
 resholds are developed in order to\ndetermine the number of doses that a
  strongly adherent patient can miss\nbefore resistance emerges. Converse
 ly\, it is shown that if this threshold\nis crossed\, even for 24 hours\
 , then resistance levels are extremely high\nand will not dissipate for 
 weeks. After this therapy interruption\, the\nminimum number of successi
 ve doses that should be taken is also\ndetermined. Estimates are provide
 d for all protease-sparing drugs approved\nby the U.S. Food and Drug Adm
 inistration.
END:VEVENT
