BEGIN:VEVENT
DURATION:PT1H
LOCATION:UNIVERSITY OF TECHNOLOGY\, SYDNEY
DTSTAMP:20080612T063022Z
UID:27C64C13-3DFA-437D-8FB4-19DD37CD7F1C
SEQUENCE:7
DTSTART;TZID=Australia/Sydney:20080725T223000
SUMMARY:TALENTED STUDENTS' DAY 
DESCRIPTION:TALENTED STUDENTS' DAY \nUNIVERSITY OF TECHNOLOGY\, SYDNEY\n
 FRIDAY\, 25 JULY\, 2008\n\nLECTURER'S INFORMATION SHEET\nTitle of talk:_
 ____The Mathematics of Knots\n\nShort synopsis:\nThis synopsis will be s
 ent out to students so that they can choose which talks to attend. Pleas
 e\nprint so that we can avoid transcription errors.\n\nThe use of string
 s\, knots and braids has a rich theory in ancient artwork\, string games
 \, fishing\, sailing\, hair braiding\, decorative architecture\, knittin
 g\, and so on. In the mid 19th century\, Lord Kelvin had an atomic theor
 y of matter where he thought the atoms were like pieces of string and th
 e chemical properties of elements arose from knotting and linking betwee
 n atoms. Mathematicians began classifying and studying knots. The subjec
 t had a revival in the mid 1980s and is a thriving mathematical research
  area with applications in chemistry and biology. My talk will introduce
  what mathematicians do in this field.\n
END:VEVENT
